<Page title="Home">
    <h1>Hello!</h1>

    <p>You&rsquo;ve reached my personal website. Welcome.</p>

    <p>
        I&rsquo;m a software engineer at <a
            href="https://www.janestreet.com/functional-programming/"
            target="_blank">Jane Street</a
        >. I love writing Haskell, but realistically most of my job is <s
            >Julia</s
        >
        <s>Python</s>
        <s>prompting Claude</s> OCaml(!). You can find me on GitHub at <a
            href="https://github.com/penelopeysm"
            target="_blank">@penelopeysm</a
        > (although note that none of my Jane Street work is there).
    </p>

    <p>
        This blog is written using Astro, with a fairly bare-bones design. It
        used to be even more bare-bones, then Claude Code came along and I
        realised I could make it look a little bit nicer without too much
        effort.
    </p>

    <h2>More about me</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Before Jane Street, I was a research software engineer at <a
                href="https://turing.ac.uk"
                target="_blank">The Alan Turing Institute</a
            > for around three years. My work there was almost fully open-source,
            and included development of various packages for numerical computing in
            <a href="https://julialang.org">the Julia programming language</a>.
            You can see a lot of this on my GitHub profile.
        </li>
        <li>
            I dabble in compilers. I&rsquo;m not an expert at all; I&rsquo;m
            just following textbooks. You can see my progress on Nora
            Sandler&rsquo;s <i>Writing A C Compiler</i>
            <a href="https://pysm.dev/cccc" target="_blank">here</a>. (Spoiler
            alert: said progress is slow.) I&rsquo;m currently maintaining <a
                href="https://github.com/JuliaEditorSupport/JuliaFormatter.jl"
                target="_blank">JuliaFormatter.jl</a
            >.
        </li>
        <li>
            At my old workplace, I worked on diversity and inclusion for around
            two years. I&rsquo;m particularly passionate about levelling the
            playing field through education and open knowledge. Amongst other
            initiatives, I developed and taught a course on cryptography as part
            of the <a
                href="https://www.turing.ac.uk/opportunities-turing/turing-summer-experience"
                target="_blank">Turing Summer Experience</a
            >.
        </li>
        <li>
            I&rsquo;m a classical musician. This blog actually started because
            at one point I decided to start collecting classical music concerts
            that I&rsquo;d attended. For some time I <i>tried</i> to talk a little
            bit about what I enjoyed (or did not enjoy) about each of them with some
            justification, although these are of course all subjective, and are not
            intended as a review of how <i>good</i> the performance was. I&rsquo;ve
            since given up on reviewing them and now just record the repertoire. Unfortunately
            I don&rsquo;t have full records of concerts before 2024. Nowadays I play
            in the <a href="https://londonrepertoireorchestra.org.uk"
                >London Repertoire Orchestra</a
            >. You should come along, it's great fun!
        </li>

        <li>
            I made <a href="https://apribot.fly.dev/" target="_blank"
                >a Discord bot</a
            > for a Pokémon trading server. I also have a <a
                href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1IR6rCNQYFccBrc_cxNVv2gEQnVpecEAo58IDRJYWNlo/edit"
                target="_blank"
                >complete collection of all (breedable) Pokémon in all rare
                balls</a
            >, which (depending on your perspective) can be either impressive,
            slightly unhinged, or both, but it kept me sane during my PhD. One
            of my first real programming projects was actually <a
                href="https://pysm.dev/hgssbf/hgssbf.html"
                target="_blank"
                >a damage calculator for the Battle Factory in Pokémon HeartGold
                and SoulSilver</a
            >. (Don't look at the code, it's atrocious!)
        </li>
        <li>
            In a past life I was a quantum chemist, which I sometimes miss, even
            though I don&rsquo;t regret moving to software engineering.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Posts</h2>
    <PostList />
</Page>
